Verdict: Accept, with two important amendments

This is the first proposal in the sequence that has found a sensible balance between ambition, evidence and delivery scope. The LRM is right to reject a full four-layer platform at this stage and convert the use case into a small number of evidence-backed Enterprise Diagnosis Briefs.

I would stop redesigning the concept now. The next uncertainty cannot be resolved through further debate. It requires testing against real records and a real decision-maker.

Why I accept it
1. It identifies an appropriate role for the data

The available data can credibly support:

discovering potential failure families
reconstructing recorded organisational responses
exposing fragmentation across business areas
identifying missing or narrow response coverage
surfacing shared dependencies for investigation
showing what is known, inferred, contradicted and missing

The data cannot independently:

prove remediation effectiveness
determine that an exposure remains active
select the best intervention
rank investments
calculate a funding requirement
prove that enterprise treatment is superior to local treatment

The revised proposal now respects that boundary.

Its value proposition is no longer:

“The model will tell executives what to fund.”

It is:

“The model will reveal important cross-enterprise patterns that may change the diagnosis, scope or direction of an executive decision.”

That is supportable.

2. The decision brief is a much better output than a dashboard or family catalogue

The most important improvement is:

The unit of output is a decision brief, not a failure family.

That resolves much of the earlier “so what?” problem.

A family catalogue would naturally become another analytical repository. A decision brief forces the analysis to state:

what was discovered
why it matters
what is fact versus inference
what contradicts the hypothesis
what decision it could affect
who owns that decision
what evidence is missing
what action is supportable now

That last distinction matters. The available data may justify commissioning an enterprise diagnosis without justifying an immediate enterprise remediation programme.

3. The revised decision vocabulary is credible

These are sensible and supportable:

commission an enterprise diagnosis
widen the scope of an existing remediation
combine planned diagnostic work
investigate a shared process or application dependency
redirect assurance
defer a local response pending broader diagnosis
consider raising an issue for an uncovered family

These are more defensible than saying the model will directly decide what to “fund” or “stop”.

Funding may follow, but only after operational experts add:

implementation feasibility
existing programme context
cost and effort
ownership
strategic dependencies
intervention alternatives
risk appetite considerations

The LRM correctly treats those as expert and business-case inputs rather than pretending they exist in the risk records.

4. It properly narrows the meaning of residual evidence

The proposed wording is appropriately careful:

later records inside or outside the response scope
mechanism components with no identifiable recorded response
hypotheses clearly labelled as hypotheses
expert-supplied options clearly labelled as judgement

This avoids both extremes:

Overclaiming

“The actions failed because incidents continued.”

Excessive caution

“We cannot establish causation, so later incidents tell us nothing.”

Later records do provide useful evidence. They simply do not prove why recurrence occurred.

5. It recognises the existing-practice baseline

The solution must outperform existing:

root-cause classifications
human-created links
thematic RCA
problem-management processes
keyword and label matching

Internal material confirms that root-cause guidance has introduced categories intended to make trends easier to identify and supports thematic examination of issues and incidents.

There is also an internal initiative focused on recurring-pattern identification and proactive problem management.

Therefore, simply finding recurring themes is not enough. The proposed capability must demonstrate incremental value through:

historical matching across classification eras
discovery across incidents, issues and actions
cross-business and cross-object matching
recovery of relationships absent from formal links
material findings not already known through existing practices

The revised baseline now tests exactly that.

The two amendments I would make
Amendment 1: Identify the sponsor before running Gate 1

The LRM says:

“Stop revising the strategy and run Gate 1 now.”

I agree with stopping the strategy debate, but not with running Gate 1 before securing the sponsor.

The sponsor is not merely required for Gate 2. The sponsor is needed to:

define which decisions matter
select meaningful seed families
record expected findings before analysis
identify what would constitute a surprising result
distinguish an interesting pattern from a decision-relevant pattern
commit to reviewing the resulting briefs

Without a sponsor, Gate 1 could become another technically successful experiment that nobody owns.

Required sponsor characteristics

The sponsor should:

Own, influence or formally advise a relevant decision.
Have visibility across more than one business area.
Be willing to document expectations before results are shown.
Be able to commission diagnosis, assurance or broader investigation.
Be willing to state whether the result changed a decision.

The sponsor need not personally control a large funding budget. They must control a real next action.

A Head of NFR, senior Line 2 executive, enterprise problem-management executive, operational resilience executive or appropriately mandated risk executive could potentially fill that role. The correct person depends on who can actually commission cross-business diagnosis in your organisation.

Amendment 2: Strengthen the Gate 2 pass condition

“At least one changed decision” is too weak.

A trivial change could satisfy it:

“I would ask someone to take a closer look.”

That would not justify scaling the capability.

Gate 2 should require a specific, attributable and consequential change, such as:

widening the scope of an existing review
adding a previously excluded application, process or product
combining two diagnostic exercises
commissioning cross-business investigation
changing the priority or scope of assurance
delaying a local response to investigate a shared dependency
initiating formal consideration of an unrecorded enterprise problem

The decision record should specify:

before: what would otherwise have happened
after: what will now happen differently
evidence: which finding caused the change
authority: who can approve or implement it
consequence: what people, scope, timing or resources change
next step: what will be done and by when
counterfactual: whether that action would have occurred without the brief
Better Gate 2 pass rule

I suggest:

At least two validated families must lead to specific changes in diagnostic scope, assurance activity, issue consideration or remediation planning, and at least one change must cross an existing business, process, product or application boundary.

Why two? One successful example may be an unusually obvious family. Two provides modest evidence that the capability can be repeated.

This is still a small pilot. It simply avoids declaring success from one anecdote.

My view of the renamed capability
Enterprise Failure and Response Intelligence

This is a good name.

It accurately describes what the data supports:

enterprise-wide analysis
failure-family discovery
recorded response reconstruction
no claim that learning occurred
no assertion that vulnerability has been proven
no pretence that the system owns an investment portfolio
Enterprise Diagnosis Brief

Also good.

It makes clear that the output is designed to support a specific decision or investigation, not to declare a final conclusion.

Recommended final use-case statement

I would describe the accepted use case as follows:

Enterprise Failure and Response Intelligence uses reasoning models to identify potentially related incidents and issues that are separated by business boundaries, inconsistent classifications or missing links. It reconstructs the recorded response through issues and actions and produces expert-validated Enterprise Diagnosis Briefs showing the evidence, contradictions, response scope, unknowns and specific decision that may need to change.

Its distinguishing question is:

Are we managing separate records that may actually represent one broader enterprise problem?

Its immediate purpose is not to determine which intervention worked or allocate funding automatically.

Its purpose is to identify when the bank may need to:

diagnose at a broader level
widen an existing response
investigate a shared dependency
direct assurance differently
reconsider apparently separate remediation activity

That is focused, distinctive and appropriately bounded.

